   MMeemmoorryy MMaannaaggeemmeenntt
       The  following options help ppaammfflliipp use memory efficiently.  Some flip‐
       ping operations on very large images can cause ppaammfflliipp to have  a  very
       large  working  set,  which means if you don't have enough real memory,
       the program can page thrash, which means it takes a  ridiculous  amount
       time  to run.  If your entire image fits in real memory, you don't have
       a problem.  If you're just flipping top for bottom or left  for  right,
       you  don't have a problem.  Otherwise, pay attention.  If you're inter‐
       ested in the details of the  thrashing  problem  and  how  ppaammfflliipp  ap‐
       proaches  it, you're invited to read a complete explanation in comments
       in the source code.



       --mmeemmssiizzee==_m_e_b_i_b_y_t_e_s
              _m_e_b_i_b_y_t_e_s is the size in mebibytes  (aka  megabytes)  of  memory
              available  for  ppaammfflliipp.  It is the lesser of the amount of real
              or virtual memory available.

              ppaammfflliipp does nothing special to allocate real memory or  control
              it's  allocation -- it gets whatever it gets just by referencing
              virtual memory normally.  The real memory figure in question  is
              the  maximum  amount that ppaammfflliipp can be expected to end up with
              by doing that.  This is just about impossible for you  to  know,
              of  course, but you can estimate.  The total real memory in your
              system should be a major factor in your estimate.

              If ppaammfflliipp cannot fit the entire image in the amount  of  memory
              you  specify, it does the transformation in chunks, using tempo‐
              rary files for intermediate results.

              In strict horizontal transformations (left  for  right)  or  the
              null  transformation,  ppaammfflliipp  never keeps more than one row in
              memory, so the memory size is irrelevant and ppaammfflliipp doesn't use
              temporary files.

              In  strict  vertical transformations (top for bottom), even when
              you allow ppaammfflliipp enough memory to keep the entire image in vir‐
              tual  memory,  it  accesses  it in a single pass, which does not
              cause any thrashing; the chunks in temporary  files  thus  don't
              help.

              The  real  memory is important when you do a column for row type
              of transformation (e.g. --rroottaattee9900).  In that case, even if  ppaamm‐‐
              fflliipp  can  fit the entire image in virtual memory at once, if it
              does not also fit in real memory, the program will  thrash  like
              crazy because of the order in which ppaammfflliipp accesses the pixels,
              and that means it will take a ridiculously long time to run.   A
              proper --mmeemmssiizzee drastically reduces the paging.

              If  you  specify  --mmeemmssiizzee too large, ppaammfflliipp may attempt to get
              more virtual memory than the system allows it and fail.   If  it
              can  get  the  virtual  memory,  but --mmeemmssiizzee is larger than the
              amount of real memory the system allows it and  the  transforma‐
              tion is row for column, it will page thrash and run very slowly.
              A value even slightly too high is the same as infinity.

              If you specify --mmeemmssiizzee too small, the program will run slightly
              more  slowly because of extra overhead in manipulating temporary
              files.  Also, if your environment isn't set up to make temporary
              files possible, ppaammfflliipp will fail.

              Doing the entire transformation "in memory" doesn't speed things
              up as much as you might think, because even with  the  temporary
              files, the data is just as likely to be in memory.  Virtual mem‐
              ory gets paged to disk and disk files get cached in memory.   In
              fact, the pixels fit much more compactly into memory when stored
              in a temporary file than when stored "in memory" because ppaammfflliipp
              uses  a  more  efficient  format.  So you're likely to have _l_e_s_s
              disk I/O when you allow ppaammfflliipp less memory.

              If you do not specify --mmeemmssiizzee, ppaammfflliipp assumes infinity.

              This option did not exist before Netpbm 10.7 (August 2002).

              Before Netpbm 10.42 (March 2008), this option  applied  only  to
              real memory.  ppaammfflliipp would always keep the entire image in vir‐
              tual memory and if it could not get enough  virtual  memory,  it
              failed.   ppaammfflliipp  accessed  the  pixels in an order designed to
              keep real memory use within the specified amount.


       --ppaaggeessiizzee==_b_y_t_e_s
              _b_y_t_e_s is the size in bytes of a paging unit  --  the  amount  of
              memory  that  gets  paged in or out as an indivisible unit -- in
              your system.  The default is 4KiB.

              This option has no effect.

              Before Netpbm 10.42 (March 2008), ppaammfflliipp used it to control its
              use of real memory.

              This option did not exist before Netpbm 10.7 (August 2002).




